From 1024707b28455dc78abccb5faff887002066c1d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Jeong, Heon" <blmarket@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 17:33:37 -0700
Subject: Fix memory leak in if-shell -F

The -F path takes a command list from args_make_commands_now(), which
hands the caller a reference, queues it with cmdq_get_command() -- which
takes one of its own for each item it makes -- and returned without
giving its own back. load_cfg_from_buffer() and cmd_confirm_before_exec()
both free theirs after queuing; this one did not, so a list given in
braces was never freed.

A configuration file of 20 "if-shell -F 1 { set -g @x 1 }" lines exits
under AddressSanitizer with

    Direct leak of 320 byte(s) in 20 object(s) allocated from:
        #2 cmd_list_new cmd.c:600
        #3 cmd_parse_build_commands cmd-parse.y:901
        #4 cmd_parse_build_command cmd-parse.y:844
        #5 cmd_parse_build_commands cmd-parse.y:915
        #6 cmd_parse_from_buffer cmd-parse.y:1057

and its arguments underneath it; none with this applied.

Applies to 3.7b unchanged; the file is the same there but for its
$OpenBSD$ line.
---
 cmd-if-shell.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/cmd-if-shell.c b/cmd-if-shell.c
--- a/cmd-if-shell.c
+++ b/cmd-if-shell.c
@@ -98,6 +98,7 @@ cmd_if_shell_exec(struct cmd *self, struct cmdq_item *item)
 			return (CMD_RETURN_ERROR);
 		new_item = cmdq_get_command(cmdlist, cmdq_get_state(item));
 		cmdq_insert_after(item, new_item);
+		cmd_list_free(cmdlist);
 		return (CMD_RETURN_NORMAL);
 	}
 
